{ f Case 1}: Assume $(a,b)\in E$ for some $b\in G$. If $b\in {m dom}(E)$, then by condition (4) we are already done. So reduce to the case when $b
otin {m dom}(E)$. In particular, by (2) and (3) we know $a,b
eq 1$, and also by (6) we know that $ab^{-1}
otin {m dom}(E)\cup{m im}(E)$ (and is not $1$). Take $c$ to be a generator of $G$ not appearing in the reduced form of any entry in $E$, and set $E':=E\cup\{(b,c),(cb^{-1},ab^{-1}\}$. Conditions (1), (3), and (5) are immediate. Condition (2) is also clear, where injectivity needs $ab^{-1}
otin {m im}(E)$. Condition (4) is also easy to check, using the fact that $E$ is injective, $cb^{-1}
eq c$, and $ab^{-1}
otin {m dom}(E)$. Finally, for condition (6), a finite check works. The main case is checking that $ca^{-1}
otin {m dom}(E')\cup {m im}(E')$. This is clear since $a
eq 1$ and $a
eq b$ (by condition (5) for $E$). One should also note that by condition (5) (and the fact that $E$ is a function), there is no pair $(x,y)\in E$ with $(x,y)
eq (a,b)$ and $xy^{-1}=ab^{-1}$, so we don't ruin condition (6) for pairs already in $E$. { f Case 2}: Assume $a
otin {m dom}(E)$. If $(x,a)\in E$ for some (unique by (2)) $x\in G$, then applying Case 1 to $x$, we reduce to the case when $a\in {m dom}(E)$. Thus, we may consider the case when $a
otin {m dom}(E)\cup {m im}(E)$. If $(x,y)\in E$ with $xy^{-1}=a$, then by applying Case 1 to $x$, we get $a\in {m im}(E)$, and reduce to a previously considered case. So, we may assume there is no such pair $(x,y)$. Fixing $b$ to be a generator of $G$ not appearing in the reduced forms for the entries in $E$, nor in $a$, then after passing to $E\cup\{(a,b)\}\in \mathscr{E}$ we again reduce to Case 1.

Let $y = (a,c,n)$. By \Cref{1516-base} (iii), we can find $c_{y,a} 
eq a,c$ such that $L_a R_a c_{y,a} = c_{y,a}$, or equivalently $L_a L_{c_{y,a}} a = c_{y,a}$. Next, for any $b 
eq a$, we can apply \Cref{1516-base} (ii) to find $c_{y,b} 
eq b, c, c_{y,a}$ such that $R_a c_{y,b} = b$; note that as $R_a a = Sa = a 
eq b$, this also implies that $c_{y,b} 
eq a$, and that the $c_{y,b}$ are distinct as $b$ variables. We now have $L_a L_{c_{y,b}} b = L_{Sa} L_{c_{y,b}} L_{c_{y,b}} a = c_{y,b}$ for such $b$, giving the claim.
